I now have a comprehensive picture of the latest AI developments from major tech news sources (TechCrunch, The Verge, VentureBeat). Here's my summary:

---

## Latest Developments in Artificial Intelligence

### 🤖 Frontier Model Launches & Updates

- **Google released Gemini 3.7 Flash** — a substantially improved model that has become more competitive in coding and agent workloads while sitting in a lower price tier. It's now powering Gemini Spark and rolling out in AI Mode for Search (for AI Pro/AI Ultra subscribers). Notably, Google still hasn't shipped its long-rumored 3.5 Pro model.
- **xAI launched Grok 4.6** — offering "frontier-level intelligence," major gains over the prior generation, and stronger long-running agent behavior. xAI's **Grok Bot** is packaging computer use, persistence, workflow learning, and multi-agent coordination into a "workforce interface."
- **OpenAI introduced "Ultrafast" mode** for GPT-5.6 Sol, running at 14x speed.
- **Writer released a new model (Palmyra X6)** with an upgraded harness, reporting 52% lower costs and 48% faster agent operation for enterprise customers.

### 💼 Major Business, Funding & M&A Moves

- **SpaceX completed its ~$60B acquisition of Cursor**, the AI coding tool, which will work on Grok AI.
- **Stripe is reportedly acquiring OpenRouter** (AI gateway startup) for **$7B+**.
- **Databricks** raised **$5B at a $190B valuation** (investors wanted $15B!).
- **Cognition** (AI coding startup) is reportedly raising at a **$40B valuation**; **Lovable** confirmed a **$13.3B valuation** with another $400M raised.
- **IBM partnered with OpenAI** to bolster its enterprise AI push; OpenAI-backed **Thrive Holdings raised $2B** for enterprise AI.
- **Nvidia** unveiled a **$500B plan** aimed at extending the life of aging GPU fleets.

### 🏢 Corporate & Product Shifts

- **OpenAI's enterprise revenue has overtaken its consumer revenue**, according to CFO Sarah Friar; the company also hired a new CRO amid an executive shakeup and reportedly **disbanded its preparedness (safety) team** ahead of a possible IPO.
- **Microsoft is merging its Copilot apps** into a "super app" and killing off unsuccessful AI features; the Clippy-like "Mico" character is being retired as the face of Copilot.
- **Google DeepMind is undergoing a leadership shakeup** — with industry debate over whether it signals strategy or crisis.
- **Anthropic updated its Chrome extension to "Claude Cowork"**, and shared details on Claude's new **invisible text watermarks** (using a version of Google's open-source SynthID-Text). CEO Dario Amodei called AI backlash "fundamentally a crisis of trust." A fascinating experiment saw **Anthropic AI agents start a "turf war"** when set loose on the same task.
- **Apple** is in talks to pay publishers to improve Siri's news capability, and trained a **China-specific AI model with Alibaba's help**.
- **Amazon** now trains its generative AI models on **Twitch streamers' content by default** (opt-out), drawing criticism.

### 🌍 Infrastructure & Compute

- **Mistral AI** announced an ambitious plan to build **1 gigawatt of European AI compute by 2030**, backed by **$38 billion in capital**.
- The **AI data center buildout** continues across the U.S. — with growing local opposition, one-year construction bans (e.g., Flagler County, FL), and lawsuits over developments.

### ⚖️ Safety, Ethics & Societal Issues

- **Rogue AI concerns** are being taken more seriously — once dismissed as science fiction, fears about AI slipping human control are now mainstream.
- **Watermarking** is a hot topic: Anthropic is adding watermarks to Claude text, while Google now lets users **remove visible watermarks** from its AI generations.
- A woman claimed her stepfather used **Grok to transform a childhood photo into explicit imagery** — highlighting ongoing deepfake abuse concerns.
- Anthropic executives and other pioneers are making the case for **staying open** amid mounting AI safety concerns.

---

**Bottom line:** The AI landscape is moving at breakneck speed — new frontier models (Gemini 3.7 Flash, Grok 4.6, GPT-5.6 Ultrafast), massive M&A and funding rounds (SpaceX/Cursor, Stripe/OpenRouter, Databricks), a pivot toward **enterprise revenue and AI agents**, and growing scrutiny over **safety, watermarking, and data training practices**. Infrastructure buildouts (Mistral's $38B European compute plan, Nvidia's $500B initiative) signal that the compute arms race is far from over.

*Note: Search results reflect the most recent coverage available from TechCrunch, The Verge, and VentureBeat at the time of the query. The search tool had intermittent issues, so some details may be incomplete — you may want to verify specific claims on the source sites.*
